# Delete rebalance whose merge-overflow split hands the parent a LONGER separator (format.md
# "Delete", the second abandon rule). Deleting from an underfull leaf merges it with its right
# sibling; the merged leaf overflows the page and is split by the balanced rule, and the split key
# REPLACES the parent separator between the pair. Here the split lands on the one oversized key
# ('017' || 1197 y's), which is 200 bytes longer than the separator it would replace, and the parent
# (a 35-record text-keyed tree at 8192-byte pages, whose interior is already near capacity) cannot
# take it: nothing on the delete path splits an interior, so the merge is abandoned instead and the
# underfull leaf tolerated. Before that rule the over-capacity parent reached the commit, which
# refused it with 0A000 and left the row undeletable. The shape is a byte contract: every core
# abandons the same merge, so the file stays byte-identical. Runs in memory and on disk.
# requires: query.select, query.aggregates, query.order_by, ddl.create_table, ddl.primary_key, dml.insert, dml.delete, types.text, expr.like, expr.text_concat

statement ok
CREATE TABLE t (k text PRIMARY KEY)

